# Copy to `.env` and fill in. The .env is git-ignored.

# ---- Which backend answers each exploration step --------------------------
# "gemini" (default), "groq", or "local". Switch to groq if Gemini's daily free
# quota is spent — Groq's free tier is much more generous and also has a vision
# model. Use "local" on a GPU machine to drop the quota question entirely.
# LLM_PROVIDER=gemini
#
# Quota safety net: GEMINI_API_KEY and GROQ_API_KEY each accept a comma-
# separated LIST of keys (wrap it in "quotes" if you put spaces after the
# commas — start.sh sources this file). When one hits its quota mid-run, the
# next key is tried, then — once the whole active provider is spent — any
# other provider that has a key. A run only ends if every key/backend is out.

# ---- Local vision model (no quota, nothing leaves the machine) ------------
# Any server that speaks the OpenAI chat-completions shape: Ollama, vLLM,
# llama.cpp. Point LOCAL_BASE_URL at it and name the model it serves.
# LLM_PROVIDER=local
# LOCAL_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434/v1
# LOCAL_MODEL=qwen3-vl:32b
# LOCAL_API_KEY=only-if-your-server-requires-one

# ---- Gemini — free key: https://aistudio.google.com/apikey ----------------
# Commented out on purpose. Left live, a placeholder key shows up as a real
# setting in every copied .env, which is misleading on a local-only install
# where no hosted backend is ever called. Uncomment only if you set
# LLM_PROVIDER=gemini.
# GEMINI_API_KEY=your-key-here
# Defaults to gemini-2.5-flash-lite (the model with free-tier quota on most
# projects; others often show a limit-0 free quota). Override if needed.
# GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-2.5-flash-lite

# ---- Groq (alternative) — free key: https://console.groq.com/keys ---------
# GROQ_API_KEY=your-groq-key-here
# GROQ_MODEL=meta-llama/llama-4-scout-17b-16e-instruct

# ---- Tier 2: which coding agent applies a fix -----------------------------
# "prompt" (default) writes a portable fix prompt and touches no source — works
# for everyone. Set one of the others to let that agent apply and grade a fix:
#   claude | codex | copilot | command
# FIX_BACKEND=prompt
#
# For FIX_BACKEND=command, give a shell template. Placeholders {cwd},
# {prompt_file} and {prompt} are substituted before it runs, e.g.:
# FIX_CMD=my-agent --repo {cwd} --prompt-file {prompt_file}

# ---- Login wall: credentials the explorer types past a sign-in / sign-up --
# Without these, a login screen is a dead end — the explorer taps the fields
# (which only focuses them) and loops until the step budget. Set the ones your
# app asks for; each NAME is matched as a lower-case substring against the
# field's on-screen label/hint, values are typed in, then the most
# sign-in-looking button is tapped.
#
# GREMLIN_LOGIN_EMAIL=auto  -> Gremlin creates a real disposable inbox
# (mail.tm) for this run, follows the "sign up" link to register with it
# (a generated name + password fill the other fields), then reads the
# verification code out of the email that arrives. On an auth screen the
# filler is the ONLY thing that acts — the model can't type a guess and
# coverage can't wander onto "Sign in" / "Forgot password". Needs outbound
# HTTPS to api.mail.tm; if that's unreachable the run says so loudly and
# skips the login. Use a literal address if your app rejects mail.tm domains.
# GREMLIN_LOGIN_EMAIL=auto
# GREMLIN_LOGIN_OTP=000000       # only if the app has a fixed test code
# GREMLIN_LOGIN_NAME=
# GREMLIN_LOGIN_PHONE=
# GREMLIN_LOGIN_USERNAME=
# GREMLIN_LOGIN_PASSWORD=
